{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793527114,"nonce":"r5Z2xsH/Lb8v9j3gNblWdAWfWb8=","message":"Linux survey (NUC x86_64, glibc 2.43, THP madvise; llvm-backend builds differing only in #[global_allocator]; interleaved runs; ratios to glibc, geometric mean over rows; lower is better).\n\nzypy speed suite as CI runs it (--fast, ZYPY_LLVM=1), 22 kernels. Score is the CI's number, the mean of the printed iteration times.\n3 runs:\n- mimalloc 0.1.52 (v3.3.2) default: score 0.956, wall 0.925, peak RSS 1.60 (worst row 1.77)\n- mimalloc with MIMALLOC_ARENA_EAGER_COMMIT=0 MIMALLOC_PURGE_DELAY=0: 0.981, 0.959, RSS 1.007\n- tikv-jemallocator 0.7 default: 0.961, 0.947, RSS 1.07 (worst 1.15)\n- jemalloc narenas:1,tcache_max:4096,dirty/muzzy decay 0: 1.021, 1.010, RSS 0.97\n- snmalloc-rs 0.7.6: 0.985, 0.965, RSS 1.13 (worst 1.34)\n- tcmalloc-better 0.1.19: 1.50, 1.29, RSS 2.05\n- rpmalloc 0.2.2: 2.16, 1.72, RSS 1.21\n- glibc with trim_threshold 64M, top_pad 16M, mmap_threshold 32M (all settable by mallopt): 0.981, 0.978, RSS 1.006\n7 runs of the low-memory candidates:\n- glibc tuned 0.994 / 0.987 / RSS 1.005; mmap_threshold alone 1.026 / 1.000; trim+top_pad alone 1.013 / 1.000\n- mimalloc tuned 0.992 / 0.970 / RSS 0.987\n- jemalloc default 0.970 / 0.938 / RSS 1.072 (worst 1.14)\n\nzylua benchmarks, 17 rows, 5 runs (wall, RSS): mimalloc default 1.058 / 1.49, tuned 1.014 / 1.008; jemalloc 0.983 / 1.065, tuned 0.996 / 1.025; snmalloc 0.995 / 1.061; glibc tuned 1.002 / 1.005; tcmalloc 1.72 / 1.59 and rpmalloc 1.41 / 1.16, both several times slower on binary_trees, records, pipeline and tokenize.\n\nSo on Linux no allocator moves the CI score by more than 1% without raising peak memory; the gains that exist (jemalloc 3%, mimalloc default 4%) cost 7% and 60% peak RSS. glibc's per-thread cache is already about as fast as the alternatives for this workload.\n\nRust allocations per run (allocation-audit build, Mac, same kernels, one run each): 0.4M to 3.6M per kernel except json_bench (44.5M, nearly all while the program runs); 62.9M over 16 kernels, of which execute 79%, optimize 5.3%, parse 4.8%, lower 4.2%, cranelift 2.3%. Compile-side counts are small; json_bench and chaos are runtime-library allocation.","files":null}]}